==============================================================================
1. Rubi's inert function heads (lowercase) in the rule files
==============================================================================
  sin[      1518
  cos[       551
  tan[       640
  cot[       133
  sec[       225
  csc[       940
  sinh[        0
  cosh[        0
  tanh[        0
  coth[        0
  sech[        0
  csch[        0

  FINDING: Rubi has six inert heads and they are all TRIG.
  No inert hyperbolic head exists (sinh, cosh, tanh, coth, sech, csch never appear as rule heads),
  so a hyperbolic integrand cannot be matched by the inert rules as it stands.

==============================================================================
2. DeactivateTrigAux's hyperbolic branch (IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m)
==============================================================================
  If[HyperbolicQ[u] && LinearQ[u[[1]],x],
  With[{v=ExpandToSum[I*u[[1]],x]},
    Sinh, -I*ReduceInertTrig[sin,v],
    Cosh, ReduceInertTrig[cos,v],
    Tanh, -I*ReduceInertTrig[tan,v],
    Coth, I*ReduceInertTrig[cot,v],
    Sech, ReduceInertTrig[sec,v],
    Csch, I*ReduceInertTrig[csc,v]]],

  FINDING: the HyperbolicQ branch rewrites all 6 hyperbolic heads onto the
  INERT TRIG heads, with the argument multiplied by I (ExpandToSum[I*u[[1]],x]).
  Sinh -> -I sin[I z], Cosh -> cos[I z], Tanh -> -I tan[I z],
  Coth -> I cot[I z], Sech -> sec[I z], Csch -> I csc[I z].
  This is the whole cross-section dependency: after it, a hyperbolic integrand
  IS a trig integrand and section 4's rules apply.

==============================================================================
3. The bridge rule — where the conversion is invoked
==============================================================================
    2  4 Trig functions/4.1 Sine/4.1.0.1 (a sin)^m (b trg)^n.m

  in 4 Trig functions/4.1 Sine/4.1.0.1 (a sin)^m (b trg)^n.m:
  If[TrueQ[$LoadShowSteps], Int[u_, x_Symbol] := Int[DeactivateTrig[u, x], x] /; SimplifyFlag && FunctionOfTrigOfLinearQ[u, x], Int[u_, x_Symbol] := Int[DeactivateTrig[u, x], x] /; FunctionOfTrigOfLinearQ[u, x]]

  FINDING: 1 rule file invokes the conversion, through ONE catch-all rule
  at the head of 4.1.0.1 — the FIRST section-4 file Rubi.m loads:
    Int[u_, x_Symbol] := Int[DeactivateTrig[u, x], x] /; FunctionOfTrigOfLinearQ[u, x]
  Its condition (FunctionOfTrigOfLinearQ) is what admits hyperbolic integrands;
  DeactivateTrig's own first clause spells out `TrigQ[trig] || HyperbolicQ[trig]`.

  PORTING CONSEQUENCE: this is a bare `u_` LHS. Our dispatcher walks the table in
  load order and stops at the first rule that answers, so the ported record must sit
  at the END of the table, after every class. Mathematica orders it last by
  specificity; we have to do it by position.

